{"parentUuid":"837805be-964d-4740-8794-6fc0c7bcc461","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"99f21099-1bdf-4e90-9d41-1443256357f6","agentId":"a37f3a34aafca097d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011SpEwfRwKffBs6mspHfVPp","type":"tool_result","content":"74\t    vId=resid[L0][1+N+10]-resid[L0].mean(0); vId/=(vId.norm()+1e-6)\n75\t    vKy=resid[L0][12]-resid[L0].mean(0); vKy/=(vKy.norm()+1e-6)\n76\t    # held-out: cue x3"}]},"uuid":"ccbbbc4b-b2f3-4d21-bd18-303bc9e4d979","timestamp":"2026-06-10T08:21:40.868Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"837805be-964d-4740-8794-6fc0c7bcc461","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
{"parentUuid":"ccbbbc4b-b2f3-4d21-bd18-303bc9e4d979","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a37f3a34aafca097d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011pmx23z54zyvo78ouB3cmq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have all the evidence I need. Here is my verification report.\n\n---\n\n# Verification report: `fra_win/summary.md` vs code + data\n\n## ✓ Item 1 — j8 headline \"FRA 0.14±0.03 vs ActAdd-identity 3.4±1.6 vs ActAdd prev-was-cue 3.0±0.8 nats @ 50% suppression\"\n\n**VERIFIED.** Recomputing `collat_at(agg, 0.5)` from `j8.json`:\n- FRA **0.145 ± 0.033** (n=7), ActAdd-id **3.370 ± 1.625** (n=8), ActAdd-key **2.981 ± 0.774** (n=8).\n- Rounds exactly to the writeup's 0.14±0.03 / 3.4±1.6 / 3.0±0.8.\n\n**Matched-suppression interpolation is genuine** (not each method's own max). `collat_at()` (`j8_pareto.py:94-100`) for each cue interpolates `np.interp(target, xs=suppression, ys=collateral)` at the *same* `target` for all three methods, and skips a curve only if `max(xs)<target` (`line 98`). So all three are read at suppression=0.5. ✓\n\n⚠ **Caveat (n mismatch, undisclosed in writeup):** FRA's average at supp=0.5 is over **n=7** cues, because \" doctor\" only reaches max FRA suppression 0.167 and is dropped by the `max(xs)<target` guard; the two linear methods saturate to ~1.0 suppression so use **n=8**. The 20× headline therefore compares a 7-cue FRA mean to an 8-cue linear mean. The effect is tiny (excluding \" doctor\" from the linear means too leaves them ~3.3/2.9) and does not change the conclusion, but the writeup presents all three as if over the same cue set.\n\n⚠ **Caveat (interp on non-monotonic x):** 3 of 8 FRA curves are non-strictly-monotonic in suppression (e.g. cue0 `0.322→0.823→0.825→0.825…`). `np.interp` assumes increasing `xp`; the tiny non-monotonicities are all in the flat saturated tail (差 < 1e-3) so the interpolated value is unaffected in practice, but it is technically outside `np.interp`'s contract.\n\n## ✓ Item 2 — j5: off-target KL 0.62 vs 7.64 (12×); held-out \" war\"×5 text KL 0.13 vs 31 (235×)\n\n**VERIFIED against `j5.json`:**\n- Part 1: `fra_offtarget_kl=0.6215`, `aa_offtarget_kl=7.638`, `ratio=12.29`. → writeup \"0.62 vs 7.64 (12×)\". ✓\n- Part 2: `fra_total_kl=0.1320`, `aa_total_kl=31.117` → 235.7×. → writeup \"0.13 vs 31 (~235×)\". ✓ (cue is \" war\", `part2.cue`).\n\n**\"Matched suppression\" is enforced in Part 1.** `j5_locality.py:83-88`: for each target the FRA suppression `fs` is computed, then ActAdd `s` is grid-searched over `[0.5,1,2,4]` to minimize `abs(sup-fs)`. Resulting means `fra_supp=0.6505`, `aa_supp=0.6470` (`j5.json`) — matched to within 0.4%. ✓\n\n⚠ **Caveat:** The writeup says the cue appears **\"5×\"** in the Part-2 paragraph. The actual `real` string (`j5_locality.py:101-102`) contains \" war\" **5 times** (\"The war… the war… the war ended, the war… through the war\") — but the cue is selected as `max([tk for c>=3])` (`line 106`), and `cue_pos` in the printout would confirm count. 5× is consistent with the text; minor point: the cue is auto-selected, not hard-set to \" war\", though \" war\" is the only ≥3-occurrence content token so it resolves correctly.\n\n## ✓ Item 3 — j7: FRA beats both linear baselines ~16× on total held-out KL (0.14 vs 2.28 vs 2.18)\n\n**VERIFIED against `j7.json` `summary`:**\n- `FRA_total=0.1412`, `Id_total=2.2784`, `Ky_total=2.1839`.\n- Ratios: Id/FRA = **16.1×**, Ky/FRA = **15.5×**. → writeup \"~16×, 0.14 vs 2.28 vs 2.18\". ✓\n\nNote: the per-row `suppId`/`suppKy` in `j7.json` are often far from 0.5 (e.g. \" war\" suppId=0.039, \" market\" suppId=0.586) because `find()` (`j7_keyside.py:83-88`) picks the *closest* grid point to 0.5 suppression, and several linear sweeps can't hit 0.5 cleanly. The \"matched 50%\" is best-effort, not exact — fair, but the linear methods are sometimes under-suppressing, which if anything understates their collateral. Conclusion stands.\n\n## ✓ Item 4 — FRA intervention = content-addressed subtraction of (q-feat,k-feat) bilinear score contributions from induction heads' `hook_attn_scores`\n\n**VERIFIED.**\n- The 4-D bilinear tensor is built by `_build_fra_result`→`compute_fra_sparse` (`fra/core/fra.py:174-175`): `int_matrix = (q_proj @ k_proj.T)/attn_scale * q_scales * k_scales`, i.e. exactly the (query-feature × key-feature) bilinear QK term, with RMSNorm correction (`line 178`). The jobs call it with `top_k=None` (all features) and `rms_activations=x_hat` (the reconstructed resid). ✓\n- `pairs_delta` (`j5_locality.py:51-55`) / `fra_delta` (`j8_pareto.py:30-39`) sum `d[\"vv\"]` (the FRA values) over the selected `(i,j)` pairs into a `[seq,seq]` score-delta at edge `(qi,ki)`.\n- The patch hook subtracts `delta*c` from the induction heads' pre-softmax scores: `s[0,Hh,:seq,:seq]-=sd` on `blocks.{L}.attn.hook_attn_scores` (`j8_pareto.py:46-49`, `j5_locality.py:63-67`). ✓\n- It is content-addressed: in j5/j8/j6 the delta is **recomputed on the held-out text** for cue-induction edges (`j5_locality.py:117-149`, \"query token==cue AND key=after a prior cue\"), not transplanted by position. ✓\n\n⚠ **Caveat on \"top-12\":** §2 of the writeup says \"take its **top-12** feature-pairs.\" This is true for j4/j5/j6/j7 (`M=12`). But **j8 uses ALL pairs** (`M=None`, `j8_pareto.py:73,83` and the docstring line 4), which is the curve behind the Fig-1 headline. The writeup's Fig-1 caption and §2 imply top-12 throughout; the actual money figure is all-pairs. Minor methodological inconsistency, disclosed in the j8 docstring but not the summary.\n\n## ✓ Item 5 — KL collateral computed correctly (KL(clean‖patched), correct sign, summed/meaned over positions)\n\n**VERIFIED.** All three jobs define `(lp.exp()*(lp-lq)).sum(-1)` with `lp=log_softmax(clean)`, `lq=log_softmax(patched)`:\n- j5 `kl_full` (`:23-25`) returns per-position `[seq]`, then `.sum()` (Part 2) or `sum − target-pos` (Part 1, off-target mass, `:91-92`). Called `kl_full(clean_logits, patched)` ⇒ KL(clean‖patched). ✓\n- j7 `klvec` (`:62-64`), j8 `kltot` (`:57-59`) identical formula, called `klvec(hclean, patched)` / `kltot(hclean, ...)`. ✓\n- Sign is correct: `lp-lq` with p=clean. \"summed over positions\" for totals, `.mean()` for per-position figures — matches writeup's \"KL on … text\" (nats). ✓\n\n## ✓ Item 6 — ActAdd baselines are genuine linear resid steers; \"cue-identity\" vs \"prev-was-cue\" as described\n\n**VERIFIED.**\n- Steer form (all jobs): `act[0,p,:] = act[0,p,:] - s*vX*act[0,p,:].norm()` on `blocks.{L0}.hook_resid_pre` — subtract a **unit direction `vX` scaled by the local residual norm** times strength `s`. ✓ (`vX` normalized at `j8_pareto.py:74-75`, etc.)\n- **cue-identity (`vId`/`vX`):** `resid[L0][1+N+10] − resid[L0].mean(0)`, normalized — the cue token's mean-centred residual at its (2nd) occurrence, applied at the cue's *current-token* positions `[1+10, 1+N+10]` / `hcue` (`j8_pareto.py:74,89`). ✓ \"subtract the cue's contrastive residual direction wherever the cue is current.\"\n- **prev-was-cue (`vKy`):** `resid[L0][12] − resid[L0].mean(0)`, normalized — the residual at **position 12 = the token right after the 1st cue** (`kpos=12`), applied at the *post-cue* positions `[12, 2+N+10]` / `hkey=[i+1 for cue i]` (`j8_pareto.py:75,90`). ✓ This is the key-side \"prev-token-was-cue\" direction, gated on the position after the cue, exactly as described.\n\n⚠ **Minor caveat:** the contrastive direction is `(cue resid − batch-mean resid)` within the single primer sequence, not a paired clean/corrupt contrast. It's a reasonable \"identity direction,\" but \"contrastive\" in the writeup overstates it slightly (it's mean-centering, not a true contrast pair).\n\n## Item 7 — Discrepancies / numbers not reproducible\n\n**Numbers that DO reproduce exactly:** the j8 0.14/3.4/3.0 @50%, j5 0.62/7.64/12×/0.13/31/235×, j7 0.14/2.28/2.18/~16×. All headline numbers check out against the JSONs.\n\n**Flags (none change the conclusions):**\n1. ⚠ **j8 n-mismatch (7 vs 8):** FRA's 50%-suppression mean is over 7 cues (\" doctor\" dropped, max FRA supp 0.167); linear means over 8. Undisclosed in summary.\n2. ⚠ **j8 uses ALL feature-pairs, not top-12** as §2/Fig-1 imply (the all-pairs run is what produces the headline curve).\n3. ⚠ **j7 \"matched 50%\" is best-grid-point, not exact** — several linear sweeps land far from 0.5 (e.g. suppId=0.039 for \" war\"), generally *under*-suppressing the linear baselines.\n4. ⚠ **\"contrastive residual direction\"** is mean-centering of a single token's residual, not a clean/corrupt contrast pair.\n5. ⚠ The \"~20×\" headline (§exec summary / Fig 1) is the ratio 3.37/0.145 = **23×** (or 2.98/0.145 = 20.5× for the key-side baseline). \"~20×\" is fair for the prev-was-cue baseline; the identity baseline is closer to 23×. Both are in-range of \"~20×.\"\n\n**Could not be independently reproduced from the provided data:** the §2/§3 qualitative numbers that have no JSON (J1 reconstruction \"~50%\", attention edge \"0.94→~0.13\", J2 single-head \"barely moves\"). These are described in the summary but the j1/j2/j3 outputs were not in the verification set, so I could neither confirm nor refute them — they are outside the five JSONs provided.\n\n**Bottom line:** Every quantitative claim tied to j5/j7/j8 reproduces from the JSON to the stated precision, and the code measures what the writeup says (matched-suppression interpolated collateral; correctly-signed KL(clean‖patched); a genuine bilinear `hook_attn_scores` subtraction vs genuine norm-scaled linear resid steers). The only substantive methodological caveats are the undisclosed 7-vs-8 cue asymmetry in the j8 headline and the top-12-vs-all-pairs inconsistency between §2 and the j8 figure — neither overturns the ~20× result."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":731,"cache_read_input_tokens":68376,"output_tokens":4373,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fet